---
title: Attributes
order: 5
description: File metadata — stat, file, touch, truncate, shred
tags: [files, stat, file, touch, truncate, shred, metadata]
---

Commands for inspecting and modifying file metadata, types, timestamps, and content.

@text
stat — display detailed file information.

@code bash
# Full file status
stat filename

# Human-readable format
stat filename
# Output includes: size, blocks, inode, links, permissions, uid, gid,
#                  access time, modify time, change time, birth time

# Custom format strings
stat -c '%s' filename             # size in bytes
stat -c '%U:%G' filename          # owner:group
stat -c '%a %n' filename          # octal permissions and name
stat -c '%A %n' filename          # symbolic permissions and name
stat -c '%i' filename             # inode number
stat -c '%h' filename             # hard link count
stat -c '%y' filename             # modification time
stat -c '%F' filename             # file type

# Useful format: all key info on one line
stat -c '%A %h %U %G %s %y %n' filename

# Stat a filesystem (not a file)
stat -f /home

# Filesystem format strings
stat -f -c '%T' /                 # filesystem type
stat -f -c '%b %a' /              # total blocks, available blocks

@text
file — determine file type by content inspection (not extension).

@code bash
# Identify file type
file document.pdf
file mystery_file
file /usr/bin/ls

# Brief output (just the type)
file -b filename

# MIME type output
file --mime-type filename

# MIME type and encoding
file --mime filename

# Check multiple files
file *

# Follow symlinks
file -L linkname

# Read file list from stdin
find . -type f | file -f -

# Don't access the filesystem, read from stdin
cat mystery_file | file -

@text
touch — create files or update timestamps.

@code bash
# Create an empty file (or update timestamps if exists)
touch newfile.txt

# Create multiple files
touch file1.txt file2.txt file3.txt

# Set specific modification time
touch -t 202401011200.00 file.txt    # YYYYMMDDhhmm.ss

# Set time using date string
touch -d "2024-01-15 14:30:00" file.txt
touch -d "yesterday" file.txt
touch -d "2 hours ago" file.txt

# Update only access time
touch -a file.txt

# Update only modification time
touch -m file.txt

# Copy timestamps from another file
touch -r reference.txt target.txt

# Don't create file if it doesn't exist
touch -c nonexistent.txt

@text
truncate — shrink or extend file size.

@code bash
# Truncate file to zero bytes (empty it, faster than > file)
truncate -s 0 logfile.txt

# Set file to specific size (pads with null bytes if growing)
truncate -s 1G largefile.img

# Shrink file to 100 MiB
truncate -s 100M largefile.img

# Grow or shrink relative to current size
truncate -s +10M file.img     # grow by 10 MiB
truncate -s -5M file.img      # shrink by 5 MiB

# Create a sparse file (allocated but no disk blocks used)
truncate -s 10G sparse.img

@text
shred — securely overwrite file data before deletion.

@code bash
# Overwrite and delete a file (3 passes + zero pass)
shred -vuz filename

# Overwrite with N passes
shred -vn 5 filename

# Overwrite but don't delete
shred -v filename

# Overwrite specific size (useful for devices)
shred -v -n 1 -s 1G /dev/sda1

# Note: shred is ineffective on:
#   - journaling filesystems (ext3/4 journal, btrfs COW)
#   - SSDs (wear leveling redistributes writes)
#   - filesystems with snapshots or RAID
# For SSDs, use: hdparm --security-erase or blkdiscard
# For full disk: blkdiscard /dev/sdX  (instant on SSDs)

@code bash
# Secure alternatives for SSDs
sudo blkdiscard /dev/sda           # TRIM entire device (instant)
sudo blkdiscard -s /dev/sda        # Secure discard if supported
